WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.842
This slide asks you to strengthen a CHECK constraint.

00:00:02.842 --> 00:00:08.842
The clinic does not allow negative standard prices, but the current threshold is deliberately too weak.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:12.902
Run the example first so you understand its current behavior.

00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:19.622
Then change the threshold from negative one hundred to zero and run it again.

00:00:19.622 --> 00:00:23.692
After the correction, the negative test value should be rejected.

00:00:23.692 --> 00:00:25.427
In this exercise, rejection is success.

00:00:25.427 --> 00:00:28.687
It means PostgreSQL is enforcing the business rule.

00:00:28.687 --> 00:00:35.187
Pay attention to the difference between fixing the data and fixing the rule.

00:00:35.187 --> 00:00:40.347
We are changing the schema constraint so future invalid values are also protected,

00:00:40.347 --> 00:00:42.107
not only correcting one row.

00:00:42.107 --> 00:00:47.237
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:53.727
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:53.727 --> 00:00:55.162
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:55.162 --> 00:01:01.472
In a real setting such as a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments,

00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:07.452
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:07.452 --> 00:01:09.652
before discussing syntax.

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:16.126
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
